	<annotations>
		<text id="0" type="text/plain" rect="(20.0, 20.0, 700.0, 96.0)" font-family="Helvetica" font-size="13">PRAKTIKUM 4 — Inference

Orange is a visual tool, not a statistics package. It shows you the SHAPE of a
variable (Feature Statistics, Distributions) and the ASSOCIATION between two
(Sieve, Mosaic), then defers every z, p and interval to the Python Script.
Knowing where your tool stops is the skill this week teaches.</text>
		<text id="1" type="text/plain" rect="(870.0, 120.0, 360.0, 250.0)" font-family="Helvetica" font-size="13">WHAT SHAPE IS EACH COLUMN?  (notebook 2)
1. Feature Statistics: look at the little
   histogram beside each column. Which are
   symmetric, which are right-skewed?

2. Distributions: 'pages_viewed'. A count of
   events in one visit -- that is a Poisson
   shape. Then 'session_seconds': the long
   right tail is log-normal.

3. Distributions: 'converted', split by
   'variant'. Eyeball the difference.
   Is it convincing? Be honest.</text>
		<text id="2" type="text/plain" rect="(870.0, 390.0, 360.0, 250.0)" font-family="Helvetica" font-size="13">IS THERE AN ASSOCIATION?  (notebook 1, 5)
4. Box Plot: 'session_seconds' by 'variant'.
   Orange prints a t-test at the bottom --
   the only test it does for you.

5. Sieve Diagram: variant x converted. The
   colour is the deviation from independence;
   the grid density is the cell count.

6. Mosaic Display: variant, converted AND
   device. Does the lift hold in both
   devices? Is the device MIX the same in
   both arms? The second question decides
   whether device could confound anything.</text>
		<text id="3" type="text/plain" rect="(450.0, 620.0, 520.0, 160.0)" font-family="Helvetica" font-size="13">ONE SEGMENT AT A TIME  (notebook 6)
7. Select Rows: add the condition device = mobile, then read the
   Sieve downstream. Now switch it to desktop and read it again.
   The association is obvious on one and invisible on the other.

   That is TWO hypothesis tests, and you chose which to report
   after seeing the data. The Python Script applies a Holm
   correction for exactly this reason. Notebook section 6 runs ten
   such slices and shows what noise alone produces.</text>
		<text id="4" type="text/plain" rect="(450.0, 250.0, 380.0, 200.0)" font-family="Helvetica" font-size="13">THE NUMBERS ORANGE WILL NOT GIVE YOU
8. Python Script: already loaded, just run it.
   It prints, in the order a real analysis
   goes: the sample-ratio check, the lift
   with a 95% interval, the sample size that
   was actually required, and the per-device
   segments with a Holm correction.

   Same file as orange/scripts/ab_test.py,
   which runs on its own with
   'python scripts/ab_test.py'.</text>
	</annotations>
